      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by understanding the geometry of the regular hexagon and the transformations involved.\n\n1. **Determine the side length of the hexagon:**\n   The hexagon has a regular shape centered at the origin with its opposite sides one unit apart. For a regular hexagon with side length \\( s \\), the distance between opposite sides is \\( 2s \\sqrt{3} \\). Given that the distance between opposite sides is 1 unit, we have:\n   \\[\n   2s \\sqrt{3} = 1 \\implies s = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify the region \\( R \\):**\n   The region \\( R \\) outside the hexagon consists of a square that includes the hexagon. Since the hexagon has side length \\( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6} \\), the side length of the square that includes the hexagon is \\( \\sqrt{3} \\) (the distance between opposite vertices of a hexagon).\n\n3. **Transform \\( z \\) to \\( \\frac{1}{z} \\):**\n   The transformation \\( z \\to \\frac{1}{z} \\) maps the exterior of the circle \\( |z| = 1 \\) to the interior of the circle \\( |w| = 1 \\). The region outside the hexagon corresponds to the exterior of the circle \\( |z| = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\).\n\n4. **Determine the region \\( S \\):**\n   The region \\( S \\) is the transformed circle \\( |w| = 1 \\) excluding the circle \\( |w| = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\). This is a lens-shaped region inside the circle \\( |w| = 1 \\).\n\n5. **Calculate the area of \\( S \\):**\n   The area of the circle \\( |w| = 1 \\) is \\( \\pi \\). The area of the circle \\( |w| = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) is \\( \\pi \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{3\\pi}{4} \\). Therefore, the area of the region \\( S \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\pi - \\frac{3\\pi}{4} = \\frac{4\\pi}{4} - \\frac{3\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\pi}{4}.\n   \\]\n\nThus, the area of \\( S \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\).",
