    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the area of the region \\( S = \\left\\lbrace \\frac{1}{z} \\mid z \\in R \\right\\rbrace \\), where \\( R \\) is the region outside a regular hexagon centered at the origin with opposite pairs of sides one unit apart and one pair of sides parallel to the imaginary axis, we proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Determine the equation of the hexagon:**\n   A regular hexagon centered at the origin with one pair of opposite sides parallel to the imaginary axis has vertices at \\( \\pm 1, \\pm j, \\pm 1i \\). The side length of the hexagon is \\( \\sqrt{2} \\) and the vertices are at \\( \\pm 1, \\pm j, \\pm 1i \\).\n\n2. **Find the region \\( R \\):**\n   The region \\( R \\) is the complement of this hexagon in the complex plane, i.e., all points outside the hexagon but inside the circle of radius 1 centered at the origin. Mathematically, \\( R \\) can be described as the set of points \\( z \\) such that \\( |z| > 1 \\) and \\( |z| \\leq \\sqrt{2} \\).\n\n3. **Determine the region \\( S \\):**\n   The region \\( S \\) is the set of points \\( w = \\frac{1}{z} \\) where \\( z \\in R \\). The function \\( w = \\frac{1}{z} \\) maps the exterior of the circle of radius 1 to the interior of the circle of radius \\( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\) (excluding the origin). Therefore, \\( S \\) is the interior of a circle of radius \\( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\) centered at the origin, excluding the origin itself.\n\n4. **Calculate the area of \\( S \\):**\n   The area of a circle of radius \\( r \\) is given by \\( \\pi r^2 \\). For the circle of radius \\( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\), the area is:\n   \\[\n   \\pi \\left( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\right)^2 = \\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\( S \\) excludes the origin, we subtract the area of a small circle of radius \\( \\epsilon \\) (for any small positive \\( \\epsilon \\)) from the area of the circle of radius \\( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\). However, since \\( \\epsilon \\) approaches 0, the area of \\( S \\) is effectively the area of the circle of radius \\( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\), which is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n   \\]\n\nThus, the area of \\( S \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\).",
