      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Denote the six vertices of the hexagon with $z_k = e^{\\pi i k/3}$ for $k = 0, 1,...5$.  Let $w_k$ be the sixth root of $z_k^{-3}$, where $z_k$ is defined as above:\n\\begin{align*}\nw_k &= \\frac 1 {z_k^3} = \\frac 1 {e^{\\pi i k/2}} = e^{-\\pi i k/2} = e^{\\pi i(-(k + 1)/2)}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSo $S = \\left\\lbrace \\frac 1 {z_k} \\ | \\ k = 0, 1,...,5 \\right\\rbrace$ is the same as $\\text{${w}_0, \\text{${w}_0}, \\text{${w}_2}, \\text{${w}_2}, \\text{${w}_4}, \\text{${w}_4}$}$}$. This is just the closed unit disk.  Together with the remaining vertices of the hexagon, we have $16$ vertices.  The region $S$ is thus a circle of radius one.  We can compute the area of $S$ by subtracting the area of the regular hexagon from a circle of radius one.  The area of the circle is $\\pi \\cdot 1^2 = \\pi$, and the area of the hexagon can be computed as $(3/2)\\sqrt3 = \\frac{3\\sqrt3}2$.  The desired area is $\\pi - \\frac{3\\sqrt3}2.$ The answer is \\pi-\\frac{3\\sqrt3}2",
